## Reviewer
### Summary

The paper proposes a multi-stage masked distillation pipeline for training self-supervised visual representation learning. The authors show that a careful choice of the target representation is unnecessary for learning good visual representation, and the proposed method outperforms previous self-supervised methods on various downstream tasks.

### Soundness

3 good

### Presentation

3 good

### Contribution

2 fair

### Strengths

- The paper is well-written and easy to follow.
- The proposed method is simple and effective.
- The proposed method outperforms previous self-supervised methods on various downstream tasks.

### Weaknesses

- The motivation of the paper is not clear. The authors claim that a careful choice of the target representation is unnecessary for learning good visual representation, but it is not clear why the target representation matters in the first place. 
- The proposed method is similar to the EMA method proposed in iBOT [1], which also uses a student model to update the teacher model. The authors should compare their method with iBOT and explain the differences. 
- The proposed method is only evaluated on ViT models, and it is not clear how the proposed method performs on other architectures such as CNNs.
- The proposed method is only evaluated on ImageNet-1K, and it is not clear how the proposed method performs on other datasets such as ImageNet-21K and COCO.
- The proposed method is only evaluated on classification tasks, and it is not clear how the proposed method performs on other tasks such as object detection and semantic segmentation.

