### Summary

This paper presents mBLIP, a multilingual vision-language model that leverages a multilingual LLM. The authors first initialize the image encoder from an English-only image encoder, BLIP-2. Then, they align the image encoder to a multilingual LLM, mT0-XL, using a mix of multilingual vision-and-language tasks. The authors show that mBLIP achieves strong performance on multilingual vision-language tasks.

### Soundness

2 fair

### Presentation

3 good

### Contribution

2 fair

### Strengths

- The paper is well-written and easy to follow.
- The proposed method is novel and efficient.

### Weaknesses

- The proposed method is not well-motivated. The authors claim that the image encoder is language-agnostic, but it is not clear why the image encoder trained on English image-text pairs can be easily aligned to a multilingual LLM. 
- The authors use a mix of multilingual vision-and-language tasks to align the image encoder to a multilingual LLM. However, it is not clear why this mix is effective. 
- The authors do not compare with other multilingual vision-language models that use multilingual LLMs. For example, [1] also uses a multilingual LLM to build a multilingual vision-language model. 
- The authors do not compare with other multilingual vision-language models that use English-only image encoders. For example, [2] also uses an English-only image encoder to build a multilingual vision-language model.

